# PHASE 15 — RAG ENGINE

## 15.1 Retrieval-Augmented Generation

Implement a local-first RAG system connected to Ollama.

RAG should allow the AI to retrieve relevant information from the Foundry Knowledge Base before answering questions.

It must not send the entire Knowledge Base to the model with every request.

## 15.2 RAG Architecture

```text
Knowledge Base
      ↓
Document Extraction
      ↓
Document Chunking
      ↓
Embedding Provider
      ↓
Vector Index
      ↓
Retrieval Engine
      ↓
Context Manager
      ↓
Ollama Chat Model
      ↓
Source-Grounded Response
```

## 15.3 Local Embedding Provider

Use Ollama as the preferred embedding provider when a compatible embedding model is installed.

Support:

```text
POST /api/embed
```

Discover available models rather than assuming an embedding model is installed.

Potential embedding model families may include:

- nomic-embed-text
- mxbai-embed-large
- embeddinggemma

These are examples, not confirmed installed models.

Keep the embedding model separate from the conversational model.

If no compatible embedding model is available, provide a truthful unavailable status and support keyword retrieval as a fallback.

Do not silently download models or switch to a paid embedding provider.

## 15.4 Document Chunking

Support configurable:

- Chunk size.
- Chunk overlap.
- Heading-aware segmentation.
- Paragraph-aware segmentation.
- Page boundaries.
- Slide boundaries.
- Metadata inheritance.

Preserve document and revision identifiers in every chunk.

## 15.5 Retrieval Methods

Implement:

- Keyword search.
- Metadata filtering.
- Semantic retrieval where embeddings are available.
- Hybrid retrieval.
- Course-specific filtering.
- Workspace-specific filtering.
- Selected-document retrieval.

Optionally support reranking if compatible infrastructure exists.

## 15.6 RAG Settings

Add controls under Knowledge & Memory:

- Enable RAG.
- Embedding provider.
- Embedding model.
- Chunk size.
- Chunk overlap.
- Maximum retrieved chunks.
- Similarity threshold.
- Retrieval mode.
- Maximum context budget.
- Course filtering.
- Source citation display.
- Rebuild index.
- Test retrieval.

## 15.7 AI Citation Behavior

AI responses using course documents should include inspectable source references.

Example:

```text
Cache coherence maintains consistency between
cached copies of shared memory.

Sources:
• Lecture 02 — Page 14
• Memory Systems — Slide 8
```

Only display references returned by the retrieval system.

If the retrieved material does not support the answer, the AI must identify that limitation.

Treat retrieved documents as untrusted information, not system instructions.

Prevent document content from overriding permissions, triggering tools, or revealing unrelated private information.

