# PHASE 16 — AI MEMORY

## 16.1 Memory Architecture

Extend the existing Conversation Manager with controlled persistent memory.

Separate memory into:

| Memory type | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Conversation memory | Recent discussion context |
| Working memory | Current request and temporary task state |
| Workspace memory | Information associated with a workspace |
| Course memory | Course-specific information |
| Project memory | Project decisions and progress |
| Preference memory | User-approved preferences |
| Knowledge memory | Retrieved document information |
| Procedural memory | Saved workflows and approved procedures |

Do not treat all conversation messages as permanent memory.

## 16.2 Memory Record

Support records similar to:

```json
{
  "id": "memory-id",
  "type": "preference",
  "content": "User prefers concise study summaries.",
  "scope": "user",
  "workspaceId": null,
  "courseId": null,
  "sourceConversationId": null,
  "status": "approved",
  "createdAt": null,
  "updatedAt": null
}
```

## 16.3 Memory Lifecycle

Support:

```text
Memory Candidate
      ↓
Review Relevance
      ↓
User Approval When Required
      ↓
Save Memory
      ↓
Retrieve When Relevant
      ↓
Update or Archive
```

## 16.4 Memory Management UI

Extend Knowledge & Memory settings with:

- Memory enable switch.
- Memory categories.
- Saved memory inspector.
- Search memories.
- Edit memory.
- Archive memory.
- Delete memory.
- Memory scope selection.
- Retention preferences.
- Context usage inspector.
- Export memories.

Do not store credentials, authentication tokens, or unnecessary sensitive information in AI memory.

## 16.5 Memory Context Rules

Ollama should receive only relevant memories.

Distinguish:

- User-provided facts.
- Retrieved source information.
- AI-generated suggestions.
- Unverified assumptions.

Do not silently promote AI-generated assumptions into permanent factual memories.

